Lets say their are 2 groups of people consisting 4 in each group playing separately. So, LAN1 and LAN2. These LANS are isolated local area networks. Using only layer 2, only these network joined by a direct point to point link using SAME layer 2 protocol could communicate.

Ethernet is a L2 protocol used generally for local networks. Long distance point to point links will use other more suitable protocols such as…PPP/MPLS/ATM.

Internet Protocol (IP) is a Layer 3 protocol which adds cross-networks IP addressing and routing to move data between Local Area Networks without direct P2P links.

IP Packets move from source to destination with intermediate networks step by step. This is encapsulated in different frames along the way. 

Routers (L3) devices, remove frame encapsulation and add new frame encapsulation every hop.

Packets - These are similar to frames, contains data to be moved and they have a source and destination address. 

But frames are generally local, while IP packets can be across the world.

While packets moving around the Layer 2 network it stays the same and are placed inside frames known as encapsulation. 

## Layer 3 Packet Structure

AN IP packet contains various different fields much like frames 

### 2 Versions of Internet protocol in use:

V4 and V6

V4 contains: 

- Version
- Header Length
- Type of service
- Identification
- Total Length
- IP Flags
- Fragment Offset
- Header checksum
- IPOPTION
- Destination and source IP address (Most Important)
- Protocol (Important) - E.g. UDP or TCP
- Data (Takes majority of the packet)
- TTL

V6 Contains: 

- Version
- Traffic Class
- Flow Label
- Payloads Length
- Next Header
- Destination and source IP address (Most Important and much bigger for IPV6)
- Data
- Hop Limit
- TTL

## IP Addressing (v4) - IPv4

IP addressing identifies the device which uses layer 3 IP networking 

IP addresses are split into different parts. e.g:

                                                    133.33.3.7

All IP Addresses have a network part             and a host part

To see if a IP address is linked the ‘network’ part of the IP address matches then they will be on the same IP address if not they are on different networks.

IP address aren’t dotted numbers. This format is created for humans to understand. Actually they are in eight bit binary numbers. So, they are 4 of these per IP address so they conclude of 32 bits in size. 

Each 8 bits is known as a octet. 

The IP address presented above has /16 prefix this means 16 bits of the IP are the network, and the remaining bits are for hosts.

IP Address are either assigned by Humans or machines (DHCP)

DHCP - Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol 

## Subnet Mask

This is very important to determine if a IP address is local to each other or remote.

It is a very critical part to IP networking and configured on a L3 interface along with IP addresses.

Most netwokr interface have a default gateway.

Default Gateway - This is an IP address on the local networks which packets are forwarded to generally if the intended destination isn’t a local IP address.

Subnet Masks - It lets a IP device know if IP address its communicating with is on the same netwokr or no. This influences if it needs to use a gateway or can communicate locally.

A Subnet Mask is configured on a host device in addition to an IP address. e.g. 255.255.0.0 and this is the same as /16 prefix.

Subnet mask is a dotted decimal version of binary number which indicates part of an IP address is NETWORK (1) and which part is HOST (0).

The staring address of the local network is ALL-0’s in the HOST part, the ending is ALL-1’s in the HOST part. 

## Layer 3 - Route Tables & Routes

This is how you send from your home device to your given destination of your choice: 

1. default route 0.0.0.0/0 sends all packers to MEOWISP
2. Router now has multiple interfaces which uses the route table for selection
3. Route table is a collection of routes it will have destination and next hop or target field. Every packets arrives it will check its destination

The higher the number is after the slash means the more specific. e.g. /32 is more specific then /0.

/32 means 1 specific IP address, while /0 means all IP address.

