## Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)

ARP will give you the MAC address for a given IP address.

For this scenario lets say we have 2 laptops both L3: 

1. Laptop 1 wants to send game data to laptop from 133.33.3.7 to 133.33.3.10 (Laptop 1 IP to Laptop 2 IP)
2. Data handled to L3 destination 3.10. It has a packet which contains source and destination IP which is the right laptop.
3. Within a local netwokr data is moved with L” Frames over L!. ARP discovers which MAC relates to given IP.
4. ARP broadcasts on Level 2 - It sends an ARP frame to all Fs as a MAC address. Asking who has Address 133.33.3.10
5. The ARP protocol sees this and responds i am 133.33.3.10 and my MAC address ends 5b:78
6. As the Left Laptop has RIght Laptop MAC address it builds a frames that uses DST (Destination) MAC obtained with ARP (Transmitted using Layer1).
7. L1 hands off raw data to L2 (frame)
8. DST MAC matches 5b:78 so L2 strips frame and passed payload to L3
9. Layer 3 sees this matches then de-encapsulates the data and hands it to the game 

## IP Routing

In this scenario using the picture above we are going to be using 3 devices

Local Communication example:

- Subnet Mask and destination IP confirm D1 and D2 are on the same network as the beginning part of the IP addresses are the same (133.33).
- So then a packet will get created called p1 withe D2 IP address as the destination
- To get D2 MAC address ARP will be used. Then that packet will be encapsulated in a frame with that MAC address as the destination

Remote Communication example: 

1. Subnet mask and destination IP show the Laptop (D3) isn’t local. Then it creates a packet called P2 that has d3 laptop as its destination IP address. This is wrapped in a frame called F2.
2. ARP is going to be used to find the MAC address of the default gateway (router 1 R1)
3. P2 with D3 destination. Encapsulates in a frame destination is R1 MAC. This is sent to the R1 MAC by D2#
4. R1 removes the frame around P2 and review packets IP destination (D3) 
5. R1 has a route table for the Pink Network which D3 is in. It will create a frame (F3) with R2 (The next IP hop) as the destination MAC. P2 is unchanged and is in frame payload.
6. F3 sent to R2 MAC address
7. R2 removes the frame around P2
8. R2 knows the destination IP of P2 as its on the same netwokr. So, it will use ARP to find the MAC address of D3. It creates F4 with D3 destination MAC and encapsulates P2.
9. F4 is sent to D3, P2’s final destination

